  <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
<% end %>

<p>
  I grew up in Milwaukee and somehow ended up in a tiny mountain town in Colorado, which feels pretty on brand for how my life has gone—unexpected turns, lots of learning, and a constant pull toward building things that matter.
</p>

<p>
  I’m a backend developer, project manager, linguist, and lifelong student. I’ve never really been just one thing, and I like it that way.
</p>

<p>
  Before I started writing code full-time, I was deep in the world of project management and IT consulting—working with teams at Xcel Energy, Sling TV, Crocs, and Sovos. I got really good at organizing chaos: Agile workflows, cloud migrations, SAP systems, and cross-functional teams with too many opinions and not enough alignment. I learned how to take something messy and make it make sense—and honestly, that’s still what I love doing now, just in code.
</p>

<p>
  Language has always been a huge part of my life too. I’m fluent in Spanish, and I spent time teaching English in Barcelona, which completely changed how I think about communication. Teaching forces you to be clear, patient, and adaptable—skills that show up in everything I do now, whether I’m building an API or collaborating with a team.
</p>

<p>
  Moving into backend development felt like a natural next step. At Turing, I leaned into building real, working applications—APIs, data models, and systems that actually <em>do</em> something. I like the logic of it, the structure, and the problem-solving. I like figuring out how things connect and making them work better.
</p>

<p>
  Outside of work, I’m usually making something—cooking, baking, experimenting with new recipes, or working on creative projects like glass etching and design. I spend a lot of time outside too—cycling, snowshoeing, and wandering around the mountains—and I’m always reading or learning something new, usually about language, systems, or how people think.
</p>

<p>
  That mix—technical, creative, a little unconventional—is kind of the through-line of everything I do. I like building things that are thoughtful, useful, and a little bit different. And I’m always looking for the next thing to learn.
</p>